\title{Rank sum examples}
\date{2026-11-25T22:16:13Z}
\tag{algorithms}

\import{algorithms-macros}

\p{Note [this is kind of weird](00U9) and definitely not an integer! So thinking of this [in terms of money](00T6) would probably not work very well. For example, consider this tree:

\diagram{100}{*}{\verb<<<|
import Diagrams.TwoD.Layout.Tree
import Diagrams.Prelude hiding (Empty)
import Data.Maybe (fromJust)

lf = BNode () Empty Empty
nd l r = BNode () l r
t = nd (nd lf lf) lf
dia = (renderTree (const (circle 0.3 # fc white)) (~~) . fromJust . symmLayoutBin) t # frame 0.1
<<<}

Its three leaves all have size 1 and hence rank 0 (since #{\log_2 1 =
0}); the internal node has size 3, and the root has size 5, so #{\Phi} for this tree is #{\log_2 3 + \log_2 5}. On the other hand, this tree:

\diagram{100}{*}{\verb<<<|
import Diagrams.TwoD.Layout.Tree
import Diagrams.Prelude hiding (Empty)
import Data.Maybe (fromJust)

chain 0 = Empty
chain n = BNode () (chain (n-1)) Empty

dia = (renderTree (const (circle 0.3 # fc white)) (~~) . fromJust . symmLayoutBin) (chain 5) # frame 0.1
<<<}

has a #{\Phi} value of #{\log_2 5 + \log_2 4 + \log_2 3 + \log_2 2 +
\log_2 1 = 3 + \log_2 5 + \log_2 3}. In general, #{\Phi} will be higher for more unbalanced trees.
}
